Found under k319 (apply worker, 2026-10-31): `_robocop_credentials_state` turns any failed read of robocop's credentials Secret into "missing", and the dry run then reports the credentials as outdated. A 403/500 is not absence (k269/k277/k319 class).

Done = only a 404 is "missing"; any other failure is reported with its cause on STDERR and counted as unknown in the dry run (test, 404 vs 403/500).

2026-10-31 (main, from k328): lib/OCP/Cmd/Keys/Secure.pm _reroll_robocop has its own `$had = eval { get Deployment } ? 1 : 0` -- the same 403-taken-for-absent shape. Fix it with this card (same lane, same file; after k321, which holds Secure.pm). With k328 a 403 fails robocop_step, and Secure then says only robocop is behind -- consistent, but the read itself should say its cause.

2026-10-31 11:17 secrets worker: cause confirmed and reproduced on HEAD+k321 (new t/329): a 403/500/transport error on the Secret read is 'missing' -> robocop_step goes for the write (PIN2 prompt, SSH token read, Secret+Deployment ensured); an operator run (SOPS_AGE_KEY_FILE) is told the Secret 'does not exist'; the dry run says 'missing, would write it'. Decision: _robocop_credentials_state reads via OCP::Cmd::Apply::K8s::read_or_absent (the role loads it cleanly: leaf deps only, no cycle) and dies 'could not read Secret ocp-system/robocop-credentials: <cause>'; robocop_step reports that on STDERR (step failed, nothing written). Dry run needs a small Apply/Drift.pm hunk (robocop_dry_run: death -> kind=>error entry, mirrors the k319 Deployment block). Secure _reroll_robocop: read_or_absent too; a non-404 stops the robocop part with its cause (the real bug there: a 500 the step's own read then got past left the running pod unrestarted, exit 0).

